Presidential elections were held in [[Slovenia]] on 11 November 2012, with a [[Run-off election|run-off]] held on 2 December.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.rtvslo.si/slovenija/predsednika-republike-bomo-izbirali-11-novembra/288076 |title=Predsednika republike bomo izbirali 11. novembra |accessdate=2012-07-25 |website=RTV}}</ref> Slovenia's 1.7 million registered voters chose between the [[President of Slovenia|incumbent president]] [[Danilo Türk]], the [[Slovenian Democratic Party|SDS]]/[[Nova Slovenija|NSi]] party candidate [[Milan Zver]] and [[Borut Pahor]] of the [[Social Democrats (Slovenia)|Social Democrats]]<ref>{{cite news|title=Pahor uradno kandidat SD-ja za predsednika države|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/slovenija/pahor-uradno-kandidat-sd-ja-za-predsednika-drzave/286810|accessdate=21 January 2013|website=RTV|date=5 July 2012}}</ref> who was also supported by the [[Civic List (Slovenia)|Civic List]].<ref name="candidates">{{cite news|title=Izbirali bomo med Türkom, Pahorjem in Zverom|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/izbirali-bomo-med-tuerkom-pahorjem-in-zverom/293862|accessdate=19 October 2012|website=RTV|date=18 October 2012}}</ref> The first round was won, contrary to the opinion poll predictions, by Pahor, with Türk placing second. In the run-off election, Pahor won with roughly two-thirds of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/izidi-vzporednih-volitev-pahor-67-tuerk-33/297155 |title=Pahorju dobrih 67 odstotkov, Türku pa slabih 33 odstotkov|website=RTV|accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
Under Slovenia's election law, candidates for [[President of Slovenia|president]] require support of either:
*10 members of the [[National Assembly (Slovenia)|National Assembly]],
*one or more political parties and either 3 members of the [[National Assembly (Slovenia)|National Assembly]] or 3,000 voters,
*or 5,000 voters.

Each political party can support only one candidate. In the election, the president is elected with a majority of the vote. If no candidate receives more than half of votes, the top two candidates meet in the second round of election.<ref>{{cite web|url= http://www.dvk.gov.si/PREDSEDNIK2007/ZVPR.html|title= Zakon o volitvah predsednika republike (ZVPR) |accessdate=2012-08-29 |publisher= Uradni list RS, št. 39/1992|date=7 August 1992 |language=Slovenian}}</ref>

==Candidates==
[[File:Glasovnica za predsednika republike 2012.JPG|thumb|200px|left|Ballot paper in the first round of voting. The sequence of candidate options was determined via lottery.]]

===Danilo Türk===
[[Danilo Türk]], the incumbent president, announced that he would seek re-election for a second term as an independent candidate and<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/slovenija/video-danilo-tuerk-se-bo-potegoval-za-drugi-mandat/279055 |title=Video: Janković podprl Türka v boju za drugi mandat :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=2012-01-14 |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref> submitted over 13,000 signatures of support to the election commission. Türk, a former [[United Nations]] diplomat and professor at the Faculty of Law of the [[University of Ljubljana]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/danilo-tuerk-predsedniski-diplomat-diplomatski-predsednik/293501 |title=Danilo Türk: predsedniški diplomat, diplomatski predsednik :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref> entered the [[2007 Slovenian presidential election|2007 election]] as an independent candidate with the support of five political parties. Narrowly placing second in the first round, Türk won the second round in a landslide, defeating [[Lojze Peterle]].

Türk was supported by two parliamentary parties, [[Positive Slovenia]] and [[Democratic Party of Pensioners of Slovenia]], and four non-parliamentary parties, [[Liberal Democracy of Slovenia]], [[Democratic Labour Party (Slovenia)|Democratic Labour Party]], [[Party for Ecosocialism and Sustainable Development of Slovenia|Party for Sustainable Development]], and [[Zares]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.delo.si/zgodbe/sobotnapriloga/danilo-turk-1-del-o-kandidaturi-in-sodelovanju-z-vlado.html |title=Danilo Türk, 1. del: O kandidaturi in sodelovanju z vlado |publisher=Delo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref>

===Borut Pahor===
[[Borut Pahor]] of the [[Social Democrats (Slovenia)|Social Democrats]] had already considered running for the office in the 2007 election.<ref>{{cite web|url= http://www.finance.si/178684/|title=Pahor pripravljen kandidirati za predsednika države|accessdate=2008-09-05 |work= Finance|date=31 March 2007|language=Slovenian}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/pahor-bi-kandidiral-za-predsednika.html |title= Pahor bi kandidiral za predsednika|accessdate=2008-09-05 |publisher=24ur.com |date=4 March 2007 |language=Slovenian}}</ref> However, he instead decided to concentrate on the then upcoming [[2008 Slovenian parliamentary election|parliamentary election]] where his party won and he was elected Prime Minister.<ref name="Ministry">{{cite web |url=http://volitve.gov.si/dz2008/en/index.html |title=Republic of Slovenia Elections for Deputies to the National Assembly 2008 |accessdate=2008-09-21 |publisher=Ministry of Public Administration |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080924020450/http://volitve.gov.si/dz2008/en/index.html |archive-date=24 September 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref> In September 2011, Pahor's government lost a confidence vote amidst an economic crisis and political tensions. In the [[2011 Slovenian parliamentary election|following election]], Pahor was elected to the National Assembly while his party lost the plurality of the votes. In June 2012, Pahor unsuccessfully ran for re-election as president of the Social Democrats. He was defeated by [[Igor Lukšič]] by a narrow margin.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.delo.si/novice/politika/sd-bo-odslej-vodil-luksic.html |title=SD bo odslej vodil Lukšič |publisher=Delo.si |date=2012-10-29 |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref> At the same party congress, Pahor announced he would run for President of Slovenia. He received support from the Social Democrats<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.delo.si/novice/politika/sd-podprli-pahorja.html |title=SD podprli Pahorja |publisher=Delo.si |date=2012-10-29 |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref> and also from the [[Civic List (Slovenia)|Civic List]], a Conservative Liberal party in the Slovenian center-right government coalition.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.siol.net/novice/slovenija/2012/09/dl_podpora_pahor.aspx |title=Svet DL podprl kandidaturo Pahorja za predsednika republike - Planet |publisher=Siol.net |date=2012-09-12 |accessdate=2012-12-04 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121103153035/http://www.siol.net/novice/slovenija/2012/09/dl_podpora_pahor.aspx |archive-date=3 November 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Pahor also submitted over 4.400 signatures of support.<ref name=candidates /> He also received open support by the right-wing leader [[Janez Janša]], which according to the newspaper ''Delo'' was the turning point in the campaign, because Pahor won due to the support of right-wing voters.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.delo.si/novice/politika/pahor-in-jansa-v-telefonskem-kontaktu-pred-prestajanjem-kazni.html |first=Zoran |last=Potrč |title=Pahor in Janša v telefonskem kontaktu pred prestajanjem kazni |language=sl |trans-title=Pahor and Janša in a Telephone Conversation Before the Serving of the Sentence |newspaper=Delo |date=21 June 2014}}</ref>

===Milan Zver===
[[Image:Milan Zver 2011.jpg|thumb|right|200px|[[Milan Zver]], SDS and NSi candidate]]

[[Milan Zver]] entered the election as a candidate of two coalition parties, [[Slovenian Democratic Party]] and [[Nova Slovenija]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/slovenija/zver-pricakujem-da-bo-konkurenca-na-predsedniskih-volitvah-mocna/278561 |title=Zver: Pričakujem, da bo konkurenca na predsedniških volitvah močna :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref><ref name=autogenerated1>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/slovenija/predsednika-republike-bomo-izbirali-11-novembra/288076 |title=Predsednika republike bomo izbirali 11. novembra :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=2012-07-25 |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref> Zver, a sociologist and political scientist, served as the Minister for Education and Sports in the centre-right government led by [[Janez Janša]] in 2004–2008. In 2009, Zver was [[2009 European Parliament election in Slovenia|elected]] member of the [[European Parliament]] as a candidate of the Slovenian Democratic Party.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/milan-zver-stajerec-ki-je-politicno-kariero-zacel-v-ljubljani/293607 |title=Milan Zver - Štajerec, ki je politično kariero začel v Ljubljani :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref>

===Failed or withdrawn===
[[Zmago Jelinčič Plemeniti]] of the [[Slovenian National Party]], who had run for office several times before, also suggested he would run,<ref>{{cite web |publisher= derStandard.at |url= http://derstandard.at/1341526764093/Slowenien-waehlt-im-Herbst-den-Staatspraesidenten |title= Slowenien wählt im Herbst den Staatspräsidenten - Slowenien - derStandard.at International |accessdate= 2012-12-04}}</ref> however, he later changed his decision.<ref name=autogenerated1 /> Monika Malešič, independent politician, also announced her candidacy.<ref name=autogenerated1 /> She later withdrew her candidacy due to lack of public support.<ref>{{cite news |title= Iz predsedniške tekme izstopila Monika Malešič, kmalu sledi knjiga |url= http://www.primorske.si/Slovenija-in-svet/Predsedniske-volitve/Iz-predsedniske-tekme-izstopila-Monika-Malesic-kma.aspx |accessdate= 19 October 2012 |newspaper= Primorske novice |date= 15 October 2012}}</ref> Marko Kožar, independent politician, who attempted to run in 2002 and 2007 but failed to gather sufficient public support both times, announced his intention to run this year as well but later changed his mind.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.rtvslo.si/slovenija/marko-kozar-odstopil-od-predsedniske-kandidature/289724 |title= Marko Kožar odstopil od predsedniške kandidature :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher= Rtvslo.si |date= 2012-08-19 |accessdate= 2012-12-04}}</ref> Other people who announced the intention to run include Miro Žitko, Ladislav Troha, Dušan Egidij Kubot - Totislo, Artur Štern, Martina Valenčič, Fani Eršte, and Milan Robič.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/slovenija/foto-predsedniska-kampanja-se-je-uradno-zacela/289746 |title=Foto: Predsedniška kampanja se je uradno začela :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=2012-08-20 |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/slovenija/za-predsedniski-stol-se-zeli-potegovati-tudi-milan-robic/289933 |title=Za predsedniški stol se želi potegovati tudi Milan Robič :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=2012-08-22 |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/slovenija/27-letna-podjetnica-nova-kandidatka-za-predsednico-drzave/290376 |title=27-letna podjetnica nova kandidatka za predsednico države :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=2012-08-29 |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.times.si/slovenija/kdo-je-predsedniska-kandidatka-fani--6e5e2e98f1-1ca741240d.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130418181357/http://www.times.si/slovenija/kdo-je-predsedniska-kandidatka-fani--6e5e2e98f1-1ca741240d.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=2013-04-18 |title=Kdo je predsedniška kandidatka Fani? |language=Slovenian|trans-title=Who is the Presidential Candidate Fani? |publisher=Zurnal24.si }}</ref>

==Campaign==
All three campaigns stated that their activities would be financially austere in accordance with the situation in the country. Each however, was marked by its own specific style. Pahor carried out a variety of voluntary jobs, and took part in a number of [[Youth work actions|work actions]] organised by his campaign. Zver participated in numerous political and civil group rallies and similar events across the country. Türk organised a large political meeting in [[Križanke]] to inaugurate the campaign, but mostly appeared at presidential events rather than ones that were explicitly campaign oriented. An important part of the three campaigns were the live radio and television debates.

===First Round Debates===
The very first debate between the three candidates occurred on the afternoon of 23 October 2012 on [[Radiotelevizija Slovenija|Radio Slovenia]]. The first television debate took place in the evening of the same day on [[Radiotelevizija Slovenija|TV Slovenia]]. Samo Uhan, a politologist at the [[Faculty of Social Sciences]] of the University of Ljubljana, stated that the first TV debate would not case a shift in support. He described Türks' performance as presidential, Zvers' as an advocacy of the [[2011 Slovenian parliamentary election|current governing coalition]], and Pahor as being burdened by his negative experiences in the [[2008 Slovenian parliamentary election|previous parliamentary term]]. Božidar Novak stated that none of the candidates were outstanding in regard to substance, but that Pahor was slightly better than the other two in terms of style. He described Türk as calm, Zver as condescending, and Pahor as likeable.<ref>{{cite news|last=Škrinjar|first=Klara|title=Prvo TV soočenje: brez ostrine, z obrisi razlik|url=http://www.delo.si/novice/volitve/prvo-tv-soocenje-brez-ostrine-z-obrisi-razlik.html|accessdate=24 October 2012|newspaper=Delo|date=23 October 2012}}</ref>

The second TV debate was organised and broadcast by [[POP TV]] on the evening of 25 October 2012.<ref>{{cite news|title=Drugo televizijsko soočenje (V ŽIVO)|url=http://www.delo.si/novice/volitve/drugo-televizijsko-soocenje-v-zivo.html|accessdate=26 October 2012|newspaper=Delo|date=25 October 2012}}</ref> The broadcast featured a number of interactive elements including a live analysis of web based reactions to the three candidates. According to this method, Pahor performed the best, followed closely by Zver, with Türk shown as the least successful among the three.<ref>{{cite news|title=VIDEO: Kako prepričljivi so bili kandidati v očeh gledalcev soočenja?|url=http://volitve.24ur.com/clanek/novicevideo-kako-prepricljivi-so-bili-kandidati-v-oceh-gledalcev-soocenja.html|accessdate=26 October 2012|publisher=24ur.com|date=25 October 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121028072547/http://volitve.24ur.com/clanek/novicevideo-kako-prepricljivi-so-bili-kandidati-v-oceh-gledalcev-soocenja.html|archive-date=28 October 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=FOTO in VIDEO: Priznavajo svoje napake, a obljubljajo boljšo prihodnost|url=http://volitve.24ur.com/clanek/noviceprvo-soocenje-predsedniski-kandidati-iz-oci-v-oci.html|accessdate=26 October 2012|publisher=24ur.com|date=25 October 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121028064540/http://volitve.24ur.com/clanek/noviceprvo-soocenje-predsedniski-kandidati-iz-oci-v-oci.html|archive-date=28 October 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The third televised live appearance of all three candidates was aired on [[POP TV]] on the evening of 2 November 2012. This broadcast had a different format. The candidates were interrogated by a journalist for 25 minutes each and only interacted with each other at the beginning of the show and between the interviews.<ref>{{cite news|title=Kandidati na Pop TV soočeni z neizprosno zasliševalko|url=http://www.delo.si/novice/volitve/kandidati-na-pop-tv-sooceni-z-neizprosno-zaslisevalko.html|accessdate=4 November 2012|newspaper=Delo|date=2 November 2012}}</ref> The above-mentioned method of reaction analysis showed that both Pahor and Zver performed better than Türk.<ref>{{cite news|title=VIDEO: Kdo je najbolje izkoristil minute na stolu?|url=http://volitve.24ur.com/clanek/aktualno/novice/video-kdo-je-najbolje-izkoristil-minute-na-stolu.html|accessdate=4 November 2012|publisher=24ur.com|date=2 November 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121105032353/http://volitve.24ur.com/clanek/aktualno/novice/video-kdo-je-najbolje-izkoristil-minute-na-stolu.html|archive-date=5 November 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The fourth TV debate aired on the newly opened [[Planet TV (Slovenian TV channel)|Planet TV]] on the evening of 5 November 2012. During the live broadcast, Episcenter conducted a study of how convincing each of the candidates had been. The results showed that Pahor was successful at convincing 45% of the viewers, followed by Türk with 37% and Zver with 18%.<ref>{{cite news|title=Predsedniški kandidati so si enotni: reforme so potrebne|url=http://www.siol.net/novice/slovenija/2012/11/soocenje_predsedniskih_kandidatov_planet_tv.aspx|accessdate=6 November 2012|publisher=PlanetSiol.net|date=5 November 2012}}</ref> The fifth one was again hosted by TV Slovenia on the evening of 6 November.<ref>{{cite news|title=V živo na TVS: Türk, Pahor, Zver|url=http://www.delo.si/novice/volitve/v-zivo-na-tvs-turk-pahor-zver.html|accessdate=8 November 2012|newspaper=Delo|date=6 November 2012}}</ref>

The final [[POP TV]] debate was broadcast live on the evening of 8 November 2012. Aside from the usual confrontation between the candidates for president it also featured a segment where the women who would become the first lady were questioned by the moderator. An analysis of web based responses indicated that Pahor made the best impression, followed by Türk and revealed Zver as the least convincing.<ref>{{cite news|title=VIDEO: Kdo je najbolj prepričal v velikem finalu soočenj?|url=http://volitve.24ur.com/clanek/novicevideo-kdo-je-najbolj-preprical-v-velikem-finalu-soocenj.html|accessdate=9 November 2012|date=8 November 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121110234221/http://volitve.24ur.com/clanek/novicevideo-kdo-je-najbolj-preprical-v-velikem-finalu-soocenj.html|archive-date=10 November 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The final [[Radiotelevizija Slovenija|TV Slovenia]] debate was broadcast live on the evening of 9 November 2012. This event also featured a segment which included the companions of the three candidates.<ref>{{cite news|title=O stricih, zdravju in ženah - kandidati so se soočili še zadnjič|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/o-stricih-zdravju-in-zenah-kandidati-so-se-soocili-se-zadnjic/295359|accessdate=9 November 2012|newspaper=MMC RTV Slo|date=9 November 2012}}</ref>

[[Election silence]] came into effect at midnight on the night of 9 November 2012 and lasted until 19:00 [[Central European Time|CET]] on the evening of 11 November when the polling stations closed.

