===First round===
[[File:Slovenia pres 2012.png|right|thumb|300px|First round winners by electoral unit<ref>{{cite web|url=http://volitve.gov.si/vp2012/en/rezultati/rezultati.html|title=Elections of the President 2012 – 11 November 2012|publisher=Ministry of Justice and Public Administration infrastructure|accessdate=2012-12-03|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://archive.today/20121228124455/http://volitve.gov.si/vp2012/en/rezultati/rezultati.html|archivedate=28 December 2012}}</ref><br />
[[Borut Pahor]] (red)<br />
[[Danilo Türk]] (blue)]]

The first round took place on 11 November. Contrary to predictions, the exit polls indicated that the first round was won by Pahor (41.9%), followed by Türk (37.2%) and Zver (20.8%).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/dvk-prestel-ze-polovico-glasov-vodi-pahor/295603 |title=Prešteti že skoraj vsi glasovi, vodi Pahor :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref> After 99.91% of votes counted, Pahor got 40%, followed by Türk with 35.8% and Zver with 24.2%. The voter turnout was record-low at less than 48%. For comparison, the first round of the [[2007 Slovenian presidential election|2007 election]] saw 58% of the electorate casting their votes and the [[1992 Slovenian presidential election|1992 election]] even more than 85%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/dvk-pahor-40-00-tuerk-35-84-zver-24-16/295603 |title=DVK: Pahor 40,00%, Türk 35,84%, Zver 24,16%! :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref>

In his first response, Pahor expressed a surprise by the amount of support in the first round and stated that he will aim to continue to be a candidate that is not limited to one side of the political spectrum.<ref name=autogenerated2>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/zver-lahko-recem-da-sem-zalosten/295604 |title=Zver: Lahko rečem, da sem žalosten :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref> Türk mentioned that the low [[voter turnout]] indicated the disappointment of people regarding the political situation and that the voters should decide what country they want to live in.<ref name=autogenerated2 /> Zver thanked his supporters and stated he was "sad" but did not decide which of the remaining two candidates he would endorse in the second round.<ref name=autogenerated2 />

In the following days, analysts expressed opinion that Pahor now has better chances in the runoff.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/makarovic-in-novak-pahorju-zelo-verjetna-zmaga-v-drugem-krogu/295654 |title=Makarovič in Novak: Pahorju zelo verjetna zmaga v drugem krogu :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref><ref name=autogenerated3>{{cite web|url=http://www.delo.si/novice/volitve/drugi-krog-karte-se-mesajo-na-novo.html |title=Drugi krog? Karte se mešajo na novo! |publisher=Delo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref> Pahor appeared to attract votes from both sides of the political spectrum, even one third of the voters of the SDS party. On the other hand, Türk got most of his votes from supporters of Positive Slovenia and from one third of supporters of Pahor's SD.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.dnevnik.si/slovenija/v-ospredju/v-drugem-krogu-spopad-na-levici- |title=V drugem krogu spopad na levici |publisher=Dnevnik.si |accessdate=2012-12-04 |archive-date=22 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150622220533/http://www.dnevnik.si/slovenija/v-ospredju/v-drugem-krogu-spopad-na-levici- |url-status=dead }}</ref> Increasing support of Pahor during the campaign was also attributed to better performance of his in televised debates.<ref name=autogenerated3 /> 
Discrepancies between the opinion polls and the results in the first round were attributed to low voter turnout, especially as a part of Türk's supporters presumably stayed at home.<ref name=autogenerated3 /> Low turnout was also interpreted as people being "fed up" with the political situation in Slovenia.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/pavliha-ljudje-imajo-poln-kufer-pribac-popotnica-pahorja-je-premierski-mandat/295619 |title=Pavliha: Ljudje imajo "poln kufer". Pribac: Popotnica Pahorja je premierski mandat. :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref>

===Second round===
Borut Pahor won the second round and the election with 67.3% of the vote.<ref name="MSN">{{cite web | url=http://news.uk.msn.com/world/ex-pm-wins-slovenia-president-vote | title=Ex-PM wins Slovenia president vote | publisher=MSN News | work=Ex-PM wins Slovenia president vote | date=2 December 2012 | accessdate=2 December 2012 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121205051813/http://news.uk.msn.com/world/ex-pm-wins-slovenia-president-vote | archive-date=5 December 2012 | url-status=dead }}</ref>
The results were first announced in an exit poll by the Mediana Institute.<ref name="WP">{{cite news | url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/europe/eu-member-slovenia-chooses-president-amid-uncertainty-discontent-over-anti-crisis-measures/2012/12/02/c969512c-3c5c-11e2-9258-ac7c78d5c680_story.html | title=Exit poll: Ex-PM Borut Pahor wins Slovenia presidential election with 67.3 percent of vote | work=The Washington Post | agency=Associated Press | date=2 December 2012 | accessdate=2 December 2012}}{{dead link|date=June 2021|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> The result was later confirmed at 67.4% for Pahor to 32.6% for Danilo Türk by the Electoral Commission of Slovenia, with 99.7% of the votes counted.<ref name="Reuters results">{{cite web | url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-slovenia-election-idUSBRE8B10DZ20121202 | title=Opposition candidate wins Slovenian presidential election | agency=Reuters | date=2 December 2012 | accessdate=2 December 2012 | last=Novak |first=Marja}}</ref> The voter turnout was 41.95%, the lowest in the history of presidential elections in Slovenia (until being surpassed by the [[2017 Slovenian presidential election|2017 election]] at 41.84%).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rtvslo.si/predsedniske-volitve-2012/pahorju-dobrih-67-odstotkov-tuerku-pa-slabih-33-odstotkov/297155 |title=Pahorju dobrih 67 odstotkov, Türku pa slabih 33 odstotkov :: Prvi interaktivni multimedijski portal, MMC RTV Slovenija |publisher=Rtvslo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref>

After the results were announced, Pahor reiterated that this "only the beginning, the beginning of something new, a new hope, a new period."<ref name="Xinhua">{{cite web | url=http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/world/2012-12/03/c_124034965.htm | title=Pahor beats Turk in Slovenian presidential election: TV | publisher=English.news.cn | agency=Xinhua News Agency | date=2 December 2012 | accessdate=2 December 2012 | url-status=dead | archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150610192955/http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/world/2012-12/03/c_124034965.htm | archivedate=10 June 2015}}</ref> Pahor stressed that people need trust, respect, and tolerance. He stated he will keep the promises he made during the campaign and will keep working to help solving the problems the nation faces.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.delo.si/novice/volitve/iz-staba-boruta-pahorja-to-je-zacetek-novega-upanja-novega-casa.html |title=Iz štaba Boruta Pahorja: To je začetek novega upanja, novega časa |publisher=Delo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref> Türk congratulated his opponent and stated he will keep an active role in the politics of the country.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.delo.si/novice/volitve/iz-staba-danila-turka-ostajam-privrzenec-tej-drzavi-in-aktiven-drzavljan.html |title=Iz štaba Danila Türka: Ostajam privrženec tej državi in aktiven državljan |publisher=Delo.si |accessdate=2012-12-04}}</ref>

